1
00:00:02,390 --> 00:00:06,010
Hi, everyone. My name is
Dr. Joseph Holloway, and in

2
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:07,750
this session, we're going
to be talking about the

3
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:11,670
bacterial and fungal causes
of infective endocarditis.

4
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:18,790
So here are the objectives
for the lecture today.

5
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:21,890
We're going to start with
some basic definitions and

6
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:25,330
classifications of
endocarditis, along with some of

7
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:28,250
the epidemiology and risk
factors associated with it.

8
00:00:28,470 --> 00:00:31,510
From there, we'll move on to
more of the microbiological

9
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:34,250
aspects of the disease,
and we'll talk about

10
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:37,230
some of the common and
less common bacterial and

11
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:41,890
fungal causes of it, along
with the pathogenesis, some

12
00:00:41,890 --> 00:00:44,690
of the microbial features
and virulence factors,

13
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:47,530
and then we'll also touch
on some of the laboratory

14
00:00:47,530 --> 00:00:49,770
techniques that allow
you to identify and

15
00:00:49,770 --> 00:00:52,510
diagnose the different
forms of endocarditis. So

16
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:01,180
what do we mean when we
say infective endocarditis?

17
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:04,060
Well, the first thing is
an infection, right? So

18
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:06,740
there have to be microbes
making their way into

19
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:10,040
the blood, whether those
are bacterial or fungal

20
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:13,620
microbes. And those microbes
are going to then cause

21
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:15,340
inflammation of the
endocardium, which is the

22
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:18,100
internal lining of the
heart. And that takes into

23
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:21,100
account the valves of the
heart as well. And it's

24
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:23,260
really the valves that are
going to be the location

25
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:26,260
where microbial colonization
is going to occur.

26
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:29,840
Now, endocarditis can
be subdivided into

27
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:31,580
two basic categories.
You have the

28
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:34,600
acute form of
endocarditis or subacute.

29
00:01:34,890 --> 00:01:37,520
Now, for the acute
form, that can occur

30
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:41,580
to normal valves or it
could occur to abnormal

31
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:43,240
valves like you
see below, native

32
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:46,580
valves or something
like a prosthetic valve.

33
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:51,040
And acute form of
endocarditis is going to be

34
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:54,320
brought about by more
highly virulent organisms.

35
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:58,100
So, classically, Staph
aureus would be a cause for

36
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:01,240
acute endocarditis. And
because of that, it's going

37
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:05,140
to show a much more
virulent, rapid progression.

38
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:07,960
Patients are going to
have higher fevers,

39
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:11,660
and it's going to last
a shorter course, a

40
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:15,860
couple of days to weeks
to show the symptoms.

41
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:19,860
And that could be
compared to a subacute

42
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:22,740
form of endocarditis
where you have less

43
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:27,140
virulent organisms
causing the pathology.

44
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:31,560
And a good example of that
would be the commensal

45
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:35,940
oral flora causing the
subacute case of endocarditis.

46
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:38,280
And so because of that, you
get a slower progression

47
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:44,160
that lasts more in the time
period of weeks to even

48
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:47,080
months. You have patients
that have lower fevers.

49
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:51,460
And one other thing to
consider is intravenous drug

50
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:55,660
use. And that can lead to
a case of endocarditis as

51
00:02:55,660 --> 00:02:59,540
well. Again, the typical
cause is going to be staph

52
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:02,940
aureus, and classically, it's
going to be associated with

53
00:03:02,940 --> 00:03:05,720
the tricuspid valve on the
right side of the heart.

54
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:13,720
At this point, I think
it's instructive to

55
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:17,360
cover the basic pathogenesis
of endocarditis.

56
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:22,940
Now, the disease has to
occur in the background of

57
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:27,720
damaged endothelial
cells or damaged to the

58
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:31,580
endothelial lining of a valve.
And so what this will do

59
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:35,920
is it will expose
subendothelial matrix proteins.

60
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:40,220
At that point, serum
proteins and platelets

61
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:44,120
and fibrin have the
potential for aggregating

62
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:47,140
at that location and
creating a thrombus,

63
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:50,140
at which point you
would then consider this

64
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:53,620
to be non-bacterial
thrombotic endocarditis,

65
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:55,500
otherwise known as
morantic endocarditis.

66
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:57,860
Now, that's not something
that we're necessarily going

67
00:03:57,860 --> 00:04:00,760
to talk about today, but it
does serve as the background

68
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:05,400
for infective endocarditis.
So you have this pre

69
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:09,820
-existing thrombus that's
sterile, and you add to that

70
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:13,400
transient microbes in the blood
that then have the potential

71
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:19,480
to also adhere to that
thrombus. Now, at that point,

72
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:21,380
you then have the bacteria
that are going to be

73
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:25,140
dividing. that is also going
to allow for the attachment

74
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:29,160
of different types of immune
proteins to that location,

75
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:33,500
to that thrombus, and the
addition of more serum proteins

76
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:37,100
and platelets. And what
this does is it produces a

77
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:43,600
large thrombus known as a
vegetation. So the vegetation

78
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:48,780
then has the ability to
obviously disrupt the tissue,

79
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:52,220
right? So you can get tissue
destruction based on the

80
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:56,320
growing vegetation at that
location. But the growing

81
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:59,820
vegetation is also then going
to disrupt the functionality

82
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:03,140
of the valve. And this is
where you get the valvular

83
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:07,020
regurgitation that's
associated with endocarditis.

84
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:11,620
Now, in addition to that,
as the vegetation is going

85
00:05:11,620 --> 00:05:15,360
to grow, there is a potential
for little parts of that

86
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:18,980
vegetation to then break
off and enter into the

87
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:21,980
bloodstream. Now if that
happens on the right side of the

88
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:25,720
heart, which is certainly
less common, right, but that

89
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:29,880
has the ability then to
cause a pulmonary embolism.

90
00:05:29,930 --> 00:05:33,680
Now on the left side of the
heart, the mitral valve for

91
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:36,330
instance, which is going
to be much more common, if

92
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:39,620
you have part of a vegetation
breaking off, you now

93
00:05:39,620 --> 00:05:44,100
have an embolism that is entering
into systemic circulation.

94
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:47,040
And once that happens,
you have the potential for

95
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:49,840
that embolism really to go
anywhere, and that could

96
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:54,160
lead to many different
complications to endocarditis.

97
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:03,780
So who exactly is getting
endocarditis? What are the

98
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:07,420
risk factors and the
epidemiology associated with it?

99
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:12,820
So certainly age is one of
the biggest risk factors,

100
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:17,500
and that should make sense.
Obviously, as people age,

101
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:20,740
there's going to be more
of a need for some of those

102
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:24,520
individuals to have
prosthetic devices, prosthetic

103
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:29,080
valves, and so age becomes a
very important risk factor.

104
00:06:29,260 --> 00:06:31,300
You can see that
most of the patients,

105
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:33,100
you know,

106
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:35,740
50 percent of the patients
that are going to have

107
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:38,100
endocarditis are going
to be over the age of 60.

108
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:42,220
IV drug use, which
we mentioned already,

109
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:45,760
is a big risk factor. Now, that

110
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:48,440
is something that's
kind of specific,

111
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:50,980
a very unique type of
endocarditis, but certainly

112
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:53,180
an important risk
factor to keep in mind.

113
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:58,400
Poor dentition or any
sort of oral manipulation

114
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:02,720
that's going to allow for
your oral flora, those

115
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:05,500
bacteria, to gain access
to the bloodstream. So that

116
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:08,540
would be an important risk
factor to keep in mind and

117
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:13,160
obviously anything that
has to do with your heart

118
00:07:13,160 --> 00:07:16,860
in terms of previous
pathology previous history

119
00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:21,540
of endocarditis maybe
rheumatic fever any sort of

120
00:07:21,540 --> 00:07:23,980
structural issue that you
have with your heart you

121
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:28,500
know for instance a mitral
prolapse or any other sort

122
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:33,460
of cardiac issue an
implantable device, if you

123
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:35,900
have any sort of cardiac
surgery, which again can

124
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:40,020
allow for microbes to gain
access to the bloodstream,

125
00:07:40,020 --> 00:07:43,780
these would all be risk
factors to keep in mind.

126
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:47,260
Now, given all those risk
factors, the incidence

127
00:07:47,260 --> 00:07:52,900
of endocarditis is
relatively low, 15 out of 100

128
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:57,360
,000 in the U.S., but still
it could be very important

129
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:01,020
to identify, especially
in certain instances,

130
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:05,400
for instance, in an acute
form of endocarditis.

131
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:08,160
And as we mentioned,
age is going

132
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:10,880
to be one of the
key risk factors.

133
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:13,580
50% of the patients are going

134
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:15,100
to be over the age of 60.

135
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:18,680
Males are going to show
this more than females, and

136
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:20,960
that could be for a number
of reasons. Maybe males

137
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:23,700
are a little bit more prone
to having cardiac issues.

138
00:08:24,220 --> 00:08:27,700
Maybe males have a
higher probability of

139
00:08:27,700 --> 00:08:30,580
engaging in IV drug
use. So there's probably

140
00:08:30,580 --> 00:08:32,960
a number of different
reasons why males

141
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:36,160
might show endocarditis
more than females.

142
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:40,640
And then lastly, the
cause of endocarditis,

143
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:43,720
the microbes that are
causing it, certainly

144
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:46,360
bacteria, are going
to be far more common

145
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:51,500
of a cause than a
fungal cause would be.

146
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,580
so having said that let's take
a look at the distribution

147
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:02,840
of microbial pathogens
that actually cause

148
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:06,980
infective endocarditis now
the first thing to say is

149
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:10,000
that you don't have to
memorize all these percentages

150
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:13,520
that you see on the screen
instead i think what's

151
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:18,220
important is that you take
a step back and what you

152
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:24,300
can see then is that the
main causes of IE are the

153
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:28,120
staphylococci, the streptococci,
and the anorococci.

154
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:31,640
Now of course it's important
also to identify the

155
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:36,840
minor causes both fungal
and bacterial and so moving

156
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:39,440
forward in the lecture what
we're going to be discussing

157
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:43,260
is how to be able to
identify a particular species

158
00:09:43,260 --> 00:09:47,440
that might be causing the
infective endocarditis.

159
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:53,640
All right, so we'll
start here with the

160
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:56,440
Staphylococci group, which
is the most prevalent

161
00:09:56,440 --> 00:10:00,180
cause of infective
endocarditis. It makes up

162
00:10:00,180 --> 00:10:03,800
probably approximately
40% of all the cases.

163
00:10:04,220 --> 00:10:07,520
Now, that takes into
account both Staphylococcus

164
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:10,100
aureus and Staphylococcus
epidermidis.

165
00:10:10,460 --> 00:10:14,080
Both of those are going to
have some common features.

166
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:16,380
The first thing to
mention about them is that

167
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:19,860
they're both part of the
normal flora of humans.

168
00:10:20,220 --> 00:10:24,440
So approximately 50% of
all people have Staph

169
00:10:24,440 --> 00:10:28,420
aureus on their skins
or in their nasopharynx.

170
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:32,320
And as the name
would imply, Staph

171
00:10:32,320 --> 00:10:35,180
epidermidis is one of
the most abundant and

172
00:10:35,180 --> 00:10:39,560
common microbes of
the human skin flora.

173
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:42,440
So virtually
everybody has that.

174
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:46,700
Now to be categorized
as a Staphylococcus, the

175
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:49,500
first feature is that you
have to be a coccus-shaped

176
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:52,780
bacterial cell, so a
circular bacterial cell. And

177
00:10:52,780 --> 00:10:57,060
those cells, as designated
by the term Staphylo,

178
00:10:57,340 --> 00:11:01,160
they are going to range
themselves in clumps. And so

179
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:04,460
the clumps, irregular clumps,
basically are based on the

180
00:11:04,460 --> 00:11:07,660
divisional plane of the
cells. They could divide in

181
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:11,570
any plane, and so that makes
the cells clump together

182
00:11:11,570 --> 00:11:15,220
irregularly, classically
known as grape-like clusters.

183
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:18,740
So they will clump
together when seen under

184
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:22,520
a microscope, and
they're also all gram

185
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:25,620
positive after a gram
staining procedure.

186
00:11:25,820 --> 00:11:29,900
Now importantly, the
staphylococci group are

187
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:32,580
catalase positive, and the
reason why that's important

188
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:35,880
is because that will
differentiate them from

189
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:40,520
the streptococci group,
which are commonly catalase

190
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:42,940
negative. So that's a
big way to differentiate

191
00:11:42,940 --> 00:11:45,800
the two different genera,
one from the other.

192
00:11:46,260 --> 00:11:49,940
Now, within the
staphylococcus group, we're

193
00:11:49,940 --> 00:11:52,780
really focusing on staph
aureus and staph epidermidis

194
00:11:52,780 --> 00:11:55,960
here. And so how
would you begin to

195
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:58,860
differentiate those? And
the first way to do that

196
00:11:58,860 --> 00:12:03,880
is with a coagulase
test. And coagulase is an

197
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:07,680
enzyme that is secreted
from a bacterial cell,

198
00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:11,620
and it's a very important
virulence factor. So

199
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:14,740
classically, staph aureus
is coagulase positive.

200
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:19,160
And what happens is the
enzyme is secreted into the

201
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:24,480
blood, and it will cause the
proteins in the blood plasma

202
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:27,820
to begin to clump together
or coagulate. And you

203
00:12:27,820 --> 00:12:30,640
could even see that in the
image of the coagulase tubes.

204
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:33,560
So you see staph
epidermidis in one, staph

205
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:36,020
aureus in the other,
and staph epi is liquid,

206
00:12:36,020 --> 00:12:39,720
whereas staph aureus
has produced a clot.

207
00:12:40,240 --> 00:12:45,880
Now, what happens basically
is the enzyme coagulase

208
00:12:45,880 --> 00:12:48,980
causes fibrinogen to
convert to fibrin, and

209
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:51,860
that will basically produce
a little clot around

210
00:12:51,860 --> 00:12:55,100
the bacterial cells. And
so really the function

211
00:12:55,100 --> 00:12:57,880
of that is to shield the
bacteria from the host

212
00:12:57,880 --> 00:13:02,400
immune response and
basically to evade

213
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:07,700
surveillance from the host
immune cells and proteins.

214
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:12,780
Now, creating that
enzyme really is a point

215
00:13:12,780 --> 00:13:16,240
of differentiation for
staph aureus and staph

216
00:13:16,240 --> 00:13:19,520
epidermidis, so that's
one virulence factor.

217
00:13:19,520 --> 00:13:21,660
Another virulence factor,
like you can see down

218
00:13:21,660 --> 00:13:25,100
in the table, is the
creation of a biofilm.

219
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:28,180
Now, the biofilm is
really going to be brought

220
00:13:28,180 --> 00:13:31,520
about by the production of
a capsule, which both of

221
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:33,820
these staph species are
going to be able to produce,

222
00:13:34,130 --> 00:13:36,480
but they produce them
kind for different reasons

223
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:38,890
and they're constructed
a little bit differently

224
00:13:38,890 --> 00:13:42,680
so for staph aureus the
capsule that it makes

225
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:48,520
again is really a defensive
mechanism to shield itself

226
00:13:48,520 --> 00:13:52,520
from phagocytosis again
to evade the host immune

227
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:56,940
response very much like
the coagulase enzyme is

228
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:02,640
but the staph epidermidis
species will produce a

229
00:14:02,640 --> 00:14:06,740
different variety of capsule
and that allows for the

230
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:12,500
adherence to different
substrates, and so it has a very

231
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:16,980
particular way of mediating
the creation of a biofilm

232
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:21,500
on different types of
indwelling devices like

233
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:25,920
prosthetic valves, and so you
have that type of virulence

234
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:28,480
factor associated with
staph epi, where you don't

235
00:14:28,480 --> 00:14:32,000
necessarily have the biofilm
creation in Staph aureus.

236
00:14:33,220 --> 00:14:37,560
All right, now, if
the coagulase test is

